window.DB_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which two properties does PostgreSQL enforce for every primary key?","o":["Stable and small","Unique and not null","Sequential and indexed","Numeric and positive"],"a":1,"w":"The database checks uniqueness (through the key's unique index) and rejects NULLs. Stability is a design choice nothing enforces."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"INSERT INTO orders (customer_id) VALUES (999) fails with \"violates foreign key constraint\". What does that mean?","o":["Order 999 already exists","customer_id must be unique in orders","The orders table is locked","No row in customers has customer_id 999"],"a":3,"w":"A foreign key requires every value in the child column to exist as a key in the parent table. The DETAIL line says exactly that: Key (customer_id)=(999) is not present in table \"customers\"."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is email a poor primary key for a users table, even if every user has a unique one?","o":["Users change their email, and every row, cache and URL that stores the key would have to change too","Text columns can't be primary keys","PostgreSQL can't index text","Emails can't be unique"],"a":0,"w":"Keys get copied everywhere. A key that changes turns one update into many. Keep email as a UNIQUE column and use a surrogate key."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the most natural primary key for order_items(order_id, product_id, quantity, unit_cents) , where a product appears at most once per order?","o":["product_id","order_id","(order_id, product_id)","quantity"],"a":2,"w":"Neither foreign key is unique on its own: an order has many lines, and a product is in many orders. The pair is unique, and as the key it also enforces \"one line per product per order\"."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You run SELECT * FROM customers twice and get the rows in different orders. Why?","o":["The table is corrupted","A table is a set; without ORDER BY the database may return rows in any order","The primary key index is missing","Another session is sorting the table"],"a":1,"w":"Rows have no inherent order. Updates, VACUUM and parallel or synchronised scans can all change the order rows come back in. Only ORDER BY guarantees one."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does SELECT name FROM products WHERE discount_pct = NULL return?","o":["The products with no discount","An error","No rows: the comparison is unknown for every row","Every row"],"a":2,"w":"Comparing anything with NULL using = yields unknown, and WHERE keeps only true rows. Use IS NULL."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does SELECT price_cents / 100.0 AS price FROM products WHERE price > 10 fail?","o":["WHERE is evaluated before SELECT, so the alias price doesn't exist yet","You can't divide in a SELECT list","Aliases must be in double quotes","WHERE can't compare decimals"],"a":0,"w":"The logical order is FROM, WHERE, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT. ORDER BY can use the alias; WHERE has to repeat the expression."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In PostgreSQL, where do NULLs appear with ORDER BY discount_pct DESC ?","o":["Last","Randomly","They are excluded","First, because NULL sorts as larger than every value"],"a":3,"w":"PostgreSQL treats NULL as the largest value: last when ascending, first when descending. NULLS FIRST / NULLS LAST overrides it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL) is true for which values of x?","o":["Every value except 1 and 2","None: the comparison with NULL makes the whole condition unknown or false","Only NULL","Only 3"],"a":1,"w":"It expands to x <> 1 AND x <> 2 AND x <> NULL. The last term is always unknown, so the AND can be false or unknown but never true."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is false AND NULL ?","o":["NULL (unknown)","true","false","An error"],"a":2,"w":"An AND with a false side is false whatever the other side is. Only when the answer depends on the unknown side is it unknown."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the size of a PostgreSQL page, the unit it reads and writes in?","o":["512 bytes","8 KB","16 KB","1 MB"],"a":1,"w":"PostgreSQL uses 8 KB blocks (SHOW block_size returns 8192). InnoDB's default is 16 KB."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly how long does one random 8 KB read take on a 7,200 rpm hard disk?","o":["8 nanoseconds","80 microseconds","1 second","About 8 milliseconds: a seek plus waiting for the platter to rotate"],"a":3,"w":"Average seek is around 4 ms and half a rotation at 7,200 rpm is about 4.2 ms, so roughly 120 random reads per second."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why doesn't an NVMe drive's \"500,000 IOPS\" apply to a single query walking a B-tree index?","o":["Each read depends on the previous one, so they happen one at a time at full latency, not in parallel","B-trees are stored in RAM","NVMe drives can't do random reads","PostgreSQL disables NVMe queues"],"a":0,"w":"The headline IOPS figure needs many requests in flight. A tree walk can't ask for the child page until it has read the parent, so it sees the queue-depth-1 latency."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A query's second run is 50× faster than its first. What is the most likely reason?","o":["PostgreSQL compiled the query","The index was rebuilt","The pages it needs are now cached in shared_buffers and the OS page cache","The disk spun up"],"a":2,"w":"The first run paid for reads from storage; the second found the pages in memory. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) shows the shift from \"read\" to \"hit\"."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On disk, what is a PostgreSQL table?","o":["One row per file","A file (split into 1 GB segments) under the data directory, made of 8 KB pages","A single file holding every table in the database","A directory of CSV files"],"a":1,"w":"Each table and index has its own relation file; pg_relation_filepath() tells you where. Files over 1 GB continue in .1, .2 … segments."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does SELECT 2147483647::integer + 1 do in PostgreSQL?","o":["Returns 2147483648","Wraps around to -2147483648","Raises \"integer out of range\"","Returns NULL"],"a":2,"w":"PostgreSQL checks integer overflow and raises an error rather than wrapping. 2,147,483,647 is the largest 32-bit signed integer."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which type is best for storing order totals?","o":["numeric(12,2), or bigint holding cents","double precision","real","text"],"a":0,"w":"Both are exact. Floating-point types store binary fractions and accumulate rounding errors in sums."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does a timestamptz value store?","o":["The local time and the zone name","A string with an offset","The local time only","An absolute instant (in UTC), shown in each session's time zone"],"a":3,"w":"The name is misleading: no zone is stored. Input is converted to UTC, and output is converted to the session's TimeZone setting."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In PostgreSQL, how does varchar(255) compare with text for performance?","o":["varchar(255) is faster because it is bounded","They're the same; varchar(n) only adds a length check","text is stored outside the table and is slower","text can't be indexed"],"a":1,"w":"The documentation states there's no performance difference between text, varchar and varchar(n), apart from char(n)'s padding."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does '{\"id\": 7, \"id\": 8}'::jsonb return?","o":["An error: duplicate key","{\"id\": 7, \"id\": 8}","{\"id\": 8}","{\"id\": 7}"],"a":2,"w":"jsonb keeps only the last value for a duplicated key. The json type would have kept the input text exactly, duplicates included."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In psql, what's the difference between COPY t FROM '/tmp/x.csv' and \\copy t FROM '/tmp/x.csv' ?","o":["None; \\copy is an alias","COPY reads the file on the database server; \\copy reads it on the client and streams it","\\copy is slower because it uses INSERTs","COPY only works for CSV"],"a":1,"w":"COPY with a file path is executed by the server process and needs server file access. \\copy is a psql command that sends the local file as COPY FROM STDIN."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In PostgreSQL's CSV format, how is the line 7,,\"\" loaded into three text columns?","o":["'7', '', ''","'7', NULL, NULL","It's rejected as malformed","'7', NULL, '' (unquoted empty is NULL, quoted empty is an empty string)"],"a":3,"w":"By default CSV format treats an unquoted empty field as NULL and a quoted empty field \"\" as an empty string."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the main reason one INSERT per row in autocommit mode is so slow?","o":["Every row pays a network round trip, statement parsing, and a commit that waits for a WAL flush","PostgreSQL locks the whole table for each insert","INSERT rebuilds the indexes each time","Autocommit disables the buffer pool"],"a":0,"w":"The actual insert takes microseconds. The per-statement overheads, especially the round trip and the durable commit, dominate."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A field in a CSV file contains a line break inside double quotes. What follows?","o":["The file is invalid CSV","COPY splits it into two rows","It's valid: one record spans two physical lines, so line-based tools can break it","The line break is converted to a space"],"a":2,"w":"Quoted fields may contain newlines. A proper CSV parser keeps the record whole; wc -l and split don't know about quoting."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why load an untrusted CSV into an all- text staging table first?","o":["Text columns load faster than any other type","The load can't fail on types, and you can validate, log rejects and transform with SQL before touching real tables","COPY only supports text columns","Staging tables are automatically indexed"],"a":1,"w":"One bad value makes a direct COPY fail entirely. Staging separates \"get the bytes in\" from \"decide what's valid\", and the final upsert is one atomic step."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which line is safe from SQL injection?","o":["cur.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '{email}'\")","cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '%s'\" % email)","cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s\", (email,))","cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = \" + email)"],"a":2,"w":"Only the third passes the value as a parameter. The second uses Python's % operator, which formats the string before psycopg sees it."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can't you write ORDER BY %s and pass a column name as a parameter?","o":["Parameters are values; the server must know identifiers to parse the statement, so the column name would be treated as a constant string","psycopg doesn't support ORDER BY","Column names must be integers","It works, but slowly"],"a":0,"w":"ORDER BY 'price_cents' sorts by a constant, which does nothing useful. Use sql.Identifier with an allow-list for dynamic identifiers."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A script runs cur.execute(\"UPDATE ...\") on a plain psycopg.connect() connection and then exits without committing. What happens to the update?","o":["It is committed automatically","It is committed when the server restarts","It is half-applied","It is rolled back: psycopg opened a transaction implicitly and it was never committed"],"a":3,"w":"psycopg 3 isn't in autocommit mode by default. Without commit() (or leaving a with-connect block normally), the transaction is discarded."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You need to export 50 million rows from Python without running out of memory. What do you use?","o":["cur.fetchall() on an ordinary cursor","A named (server-side) cursor, which fetches rows in batches","cur.fetchone() in a loop on an ordinary cursor","A larger LIMIT"],"a":1,"w":"An ordinary cursor receives the whole result when execute() returns, whatever fetch method you call afterwards. A named cursor keeps it on the server. COPY TO STDOUT is the other good option."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does the input x' OR '1'='1 do when formatted into WHERE email = '{input}' ?","o":["Matches nothing","Raises a syntax error","Makes the condition true for every row, so every customer is returned","Deletes the table"],"a":2,"w":"The query becomes WHERE email = 'x' OR '1'='1', and '1'='1' is always true. With a parameter, the whole input is one string compared with email and matches nothing."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which SQL corresponds to the projection π dept_id (employees) in relational algebra?","o":["SELECT dept_id FROM employees","SELECT DISTINCT dept_id FROM employees","SELECT * FROM employees WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL","SELECT count(dept_id) FROM employees"],"a":1,"w":"Algebraic projection returns a set, so duplicates are removed. Plain SELECT keeps duplicates (bag semantics)."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"employees has 1,000 rows and departments has 20. How many rows does SELECT * FROM employees, departments return with no WHERE clause?","o":["1,000","1,020","20","20,000: a Cartesian product"],"a":3,"w":"Listing tables with commas and no join condition is R × S: every employee paired with every department."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which query lists customers who ordered in 2025 but not in 2026?","o":["The 2025 customer IDs EXCEPT the 2026 customer IDs","The 2025 IDs INTERSECT the 2026 IDs","The 2025 IDs UNION the 2026 IDs","The 2025 IDs UNION ALL the 2026 IDs"],"a":0,"w":"Difference (R − S, EXCEPT in SQL) keeps rows of the first input that don't appear in the second."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is UNION ALL usually faster than UNION ?","o":["It uses an index","It runs the two queries in parallel","It doesn't have to sort or hash all rows to remove duplicates","It returns fewer rows"],"a":2,"w":"UNION must find and remove duplicates across the combined result. UNION ALL just concatenates."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does \"pushing a selection below a join\" mean?","o":["Running the WHERE clause after the SELECT list","Applying a filter that uses only one input's columns to that input before joining, so the join processes fewer rows","Replacing the join with a subquery","Adding an index on the join column"],"a":1,"w":"σ p (R ⋈ S) = R ⋈ σ p (S) when p mentions only S (for inner joins). Same result, smaller intermediate data."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What fixed overhead does every PostgreSQL row carry before any column data?","o":["None","8 bytes","A 23-byte tuple header (padded to 24) plus a 4-byte line pointer","One full 8 KB page"],"a":2,"w":"The header holds MVCC information; the line pointer lives in the page's slot array. Together about 28 bytes per row."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Columns declared as (boolean, bigint, boolean, bigint) waste space because…","o":["each bigint must start at a multiple of 8, so 7 padding bytes follow each boolean","booleans are stored as 8 bytes","PostgreSQL stores booleans in a separate file","bigints are compressed"],"a":0,"w":"Alignment padding. Declaring the bigints first and the booleans after removes it."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Rows are 76 bytes including the line pointer. Roughly how many fit on an 8 KB PostgreSQL page?","o":["8","54","1,000","About 107"],"a":3,"w":"8,168 usable bytes ÷ 76 ≈ 107."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly how big is a B-tree index on a bigint column for 100 million rows?","o":["About 22 MB","About 2.2 GB","About 800 MB, exactly 8 bytes per row","About 22 GB"],"a":1,"w":"Each entry is about 20 bytes (8-byte index tuple header, 8-byte key, 4-byte line pointer), pages are 90% full: around 22 MB per million rows."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A table is 300 GB and the server has 128 GB of RAM. Does it matter?","o":["Yes: the whole table must fit in RAM to be queried","No: RAM size never matters for databases","It depends on the working set: if queries mostly touch recent rows and index upper levels that fit in RAM, it can perform well","Only if it has no indexes"],"a":2,"w":"Databases cache pages, not tables. What matters is whether the pages your queries actually touch stay cached."},{"n":1,"ti":"The Relational Model","f":"01-relational-model.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In a navigational (IMS or CODASYL) database, how did a program find related records?","o":["By writing SQL joins","By full-text search","By following stored pointers along access paths built into the database","By hashing primary keys"],"a":2,"w":"Programs walked from parent to child records along predefined paths, so they depended on how the data was stored."},{"n":1,"ti":"The Relational Model","f":"01-relational-model.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You add an index and a report gets 100× faster with no code change. Which property is that?","o":["Physical data independence","Logical data independence","Referential integrity","Durability"],"a":0,"w":"A change to storage and access paths left the query unchanged; the planner simply chose a better plan."},{"n":1,"ti":"The Relational Model","f":"01-relational-model.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main cost of SQL being declarative?","o":["Queries must be rewritten when data grows","You can't join tables","Queries are always slower than hand-written code","You give up direct control of the plan, so a bad estimate can produce a slow plan you have to diagnose"],"a":3,"w":"The planner usually chooses well, but when its statistics mislead it you need EXPLAIN to see what it did and why."},{"n":1,"ti":"The Relational Model","f":"01-relational-model.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which statement is DDL?","o":["UPDATE orders SET status = 'paid' WHERE order_id = 7","ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN paid_at timestamptz","SELECT count(*) FROM orders","DELETE FROM orders WHERE order_id = 7"],"a":1,"w":"DDL (CREATE, ALTER, DROP) changes the schema. UPDATE, DELETE and INSERT are DML and change the instance."},{"n":1,"ti":"The Relational Model","f":"01-relational-model.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Where does PostgreSQL keep the definitions of your tables and columns?","o":["In a configuration file next to the data directory","Only in the application's migration files","In system catalog tables (pg_class, pg_attribute…), queryable with SQL and via information_schema","In the WAL"],"a":2,"w":"The catalog is itself relational. information_schema is the SQL-standard view over it; pg_class also holds planner statistics like reltuples."},{"n":2,"ti":"Filtering, Sorting & Pagination","f":"02-filtering-sorting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which condition selects exactly the orders placed in September 2026 (created_at is a timestamptz)?","o":["created_at BETWEEN '2026-09-01' AND '2026-09-30'","created_at >= '2026-09-01' AND created_at < '2026-10-01'","created_at BETWEEN '2026-09-01' AND '2026-10-01'","created_at > '2026-09-01' AND created_at <= '2026-09-30'"],"a":1,"w":"A half-open range includes all of 30 September and excludes midnight on 1 October. BETWEEN … '2026-09-30' stops at the start of the 30th."},{"n":2,"ti":"Filtering, Sorting & Pagination","f":"02-filtering-sorting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"There is an index on orders(created_at) . Which filter can't use it?","o":["created_at >= '2026-09-30'","created_at < now() - interval '1 day'","created_at BETWEEN $1 AND $2","created_at::date = '2026-09-30'"],"a":3,"w":"The cast wraps the column, so the planner would have to compute it for every row. Rewrite it as a range on the bare column."},{"n":2,"ti":"Filtering, Sorting & Pagination","f":"02-filtering-sorting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A feed uses ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 20 . Five new posts arrive between the user loading page 1 and page 2. What do they see on page 2?","o":["The last five posts of page 1 again, then fifteen new-to-them posts","Exactly the next twenty posts","The five new posts","An error"],"a":0,"w":"Everything shifted down by five, so offset 20 now starts five rows earlier in what the user had already seen. Keyset pagination doesn't drift."},{"n":2,"ti":"Filtering, Sorting & Pagination","f":"02-filtering-sorting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why add post_id after created_at in a paginated ORDER BY?","o":["It makes the sort faster","PostgreSQL requires a primary key in ORDER BY","It breaks ties, so rows with the same created_at have a fixed order and can't repeat or vanish across pages","It removes duplicate posts"],"a":2,"w":"Without a unique tie-breaker the order among equal values is undefined and can change between queries."},{"n":2,"ti":"Filtering, Sorting & Pagination","f":"02-filtering-sorting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does keyset pagination give up compared with OFFSET?","o":["Correct ordering","Jumping straight to an arbitrary page number","The ability to use indexes","Page sizes larger than 10"],"a":1,"w":"Keyset pagination can only continue from a known position (next or previous). For infinite scroll and APIs that's fine; for \"go to page 37\" it isn't."},{"n":3,"ti":"Joins","f":"03-joins.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"customers has 4 rows; one customer has no orders. orders has 5 rows, all with a valid customer except one guest order (customer_id NULL). How many rows does customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id return?","o":["4","5","5: the 4 matched orders plus 1 row for the customer with none","6"],"a":2,"w":"The four orders with customers each produce a row, the customer with no orders adds one NULL-padded row, and the guest order (right side, unmatched) is dropped by a LEFT join: 5 rows."},{"n":3,"ti":"Joins","f":"03-joins.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does adding WHERE o.status = 'paid' to customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON … do?","o":["Removes customers without paid orders, effectively making it an inner join","Keeps all customers and shows only paid orders","Raises an error","Nothing; WHERE and ON are equivalent"],"a":0,"w":"For unmatched customers o.status is NULL, the comparison is unknown, and WHERE drops the row. Put the condition in ON to keep them."},{"n":3,"ti":"Joins","f":"03-joins.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"An order of £100 has two payment rows. A query joins orders to payments and computes SUM(o.total) . What does it report for that order?","o":["£100","£50","An error","£200, because the order row appears once per payment"],"a":3,"w":"Fan-out: the one-to-many join repeats the order row for each payment. Aggregate payments per order first, or don't join them."},{"n":3,"ti":"Joins","f":"03-joins.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In a self join listing pairs of users from the same city, why add b.user_id > a.user_id ?","o":["To make the join use an index","To exclude pairing a user with themselves and to list each pair once instead of twice","To sort the output","It's required syntax for self joins"],"a":1,"w":"Without it you'd get (Ana, Ana) and both (Ana, Tom) and (Tom, Ana). The strict inequality keeps exactly one ordering of each pair."},{"n":3,"ti":"Joins","f":"03-joins.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"After customers LEFT JOIN orders , which expression correctly counts each customer's orders, giving 0 for customers with none?","o":["COUNT(*)","COUNT(c.customer_id)","COUNT(o.order_id)","SUM(1)"],"a":2,"w":"COUNT(column) skips NULLs, and unmatched customers have o.order_id NULL. COUNT(*) counts the NULL-padded row as 1."},{"n":4,"ti":"GROUP BY & Aggregates","f":"04-group-by-aggregates.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A column holds 10, NULL, 10, 30. What is AVG(col) ?","o":["12.5","NULL","16.67: the NULL is ignored, so it's 50 ÷ 3","20"],"a":2,"w":"Aggregates skip NULLs: the sum is 50 over 3 non-NULL values."},{"n":4,"ti":"GROUP BY & Aggregates","f":"04-group-by-aggregates.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does SELECT country, customer_id, count(*) FROM orders GROUP BY country fail?","o":["customer_id can have many values within one country group, so there's no single value to show","count(*) can't be used with GROUP BY","country must be aggregated","GROUP BY must list every column in the table"],"a":0,"w":"Each output row represents a group. Only grouped columns and aggregates (or columns dependent on a grouped primary key) have one value per group."},{"n":4,"ti":"GROUP BY & Aggregates","f":"04-group-by-aggregates.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which query finds countries with at least 100 paid orders?","o":["… WHERE count(*) >= 100 GROUP BY country","… WHERE status = 'paid' AND count(*) >= 100 GROUP BY country","… GROUP BY country HAVING status = 'paid'","… WHERE status = 'paid' GROUP BY country HAVING count(*) >= 100"],"a":3,"w":"The row condition (paid) goes in WHERE; the condition on the aggregate goes in HAVING."},{"n":4,"ti":"GROUP BY & Aggregates","f":"04-group-by-aggregates.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does SUM(amount) return when no rows match the WHERE clause?","o":["0","NULL","An error","No row at all"],"a":1,"w":"An aggregate without GROUP BY always returns one row; SUM over zero rows is NULL. COUNT would be 0. Use coalesce(sum(amount), 0)."},{"n":4,"ti":"GROUP BY & Aggregates","f":"04-group-by-aggregates.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does GROUP BY ROLLUP (country, status) produce?","o":["Only (country, status) groups","Every combination, including (status) alone","(country, status) groups, per-country subtotals, and a grand total","A pivot table with one column per status"],"a":2,"w":"ROLLUP(a, b) is GROUPING SETS ((a, b), (a), ()). CUBE would add (b) on its own."},{"n":5,"ti":"Subqueries & CTEs","f":"05-subqueries-ctes.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What happens if a scalar subquery used in a WHERE comparison returns two rows?","o":["The first row is used","The statement fails: more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression","The comparison becomes NULL","Both rows are compared, like IN"],"a":1,"w":"A scalar subquery must produce at most one row. Zero rows gives NULL; more than one is a runtime error."},{"n":5,"ti":"Subqueries & CTEs","f":"05-subqueries-ctes.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What makes a subquery \"correlated\"?","o":["It uses an aggregate","It appears in the FROM clause","It returns more than one column","It refers to a column of the outer query, so logically it's evaluated per outer row"],"a":3,"w":"For example, WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id inside a subquery over orders, where c belongs to the outer query."},{"n":5,"ti":"Subqueries & CTEs","f":"05-subqueries-ctes.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When does a recursive CTE stop?","o":["When an iteration of the recursive term produces no new rows","After a fixed 100 iterations","When the anchor returns no rows","When it reaches the root of the tree"],"a":0,"w":"Each iteration processes the previous iteration's rows. An empty iteration ends it. A cycle means that never happens, hence CYCLE guards or depth limits."},{"n":5,"ti":"Subqueries & CTEs","f":"05-subqueries-ctes.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Since PostgreSQL 12, what happens to a non-recursive CTE that's referenced exactly once?","o":["It's always materialised to a temporary table first","It's executed twice","It's inlined into the main query, so outer filters can be pushed into it (unless written AS MATERIALIZED)","It's cached across sessions"],"a":2,"w":"Before version 12 CTEs were optimisation fences. Now single-use, side-effect-free CTEs are inlined; multi-use ones are still computed once."},{"n":5,"ti":"Subqueries & CTEs","f":"05-subqueries-ctes.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You want each customer's 3 most recent orders, and customers have thousands of orders each. Which form is efficient with an index on orders(customer_id, created_at)?","o":["A correlated scalar subquery","CROSS JOIN LATERAL (… ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 3)","A recursive CTE","GROUP BY customer_id with max(created_at)"],"a":1,"w":"LATERAL runs a short index scan per customer and stops after 3 rows. A window function would number every order of every customer first."},{"n":6,"ti":"Window Functions","f":"06-window-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Amounts in descending order are 300, 200, 200, 100. What does DENSE_RANK() give the row with 100?","o":["4","2","3","1"],"a":2,"w":"DENSE_RANK doesn't leave gaps after ties: 1, 2, 2, 3. RANK would give 4."},{"n":6,"ti":"Window Functions","f":"06-window-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can't you write WHERE row_number() OVER (…) <= 3 ?","o":["Window functions are computed after WHERE, so their results don't exist yet when WHERE runs","row_number needs GROUP BY","Window functions can only appear in ORDER BY","You can; it's valid SQL"],"a":0,"w":"Compute the window function in a subquery or CTE, then filter on its result in the outer query."},{"n":6,"ti":"Window Functions","f":"06-window-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two rows share the same month. With sum(amount) OVER (ORDER BY month) , what is the running total on the first of them?","o":["Everything before it plus its own amount only","Its own amount only","The total of the whole table","Everything up to and including both rows of that month, because the default RANGE frame includes peers"],"a":3,"w":"The default frame is RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW, which treats rows with equal ORDER BY values as one step. Use ROWS for a row-by-row running total."},{"n":6,"ti":"Window Functions","f":"06-window-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does lag(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) return for the earliest month?","o":["0","NULL, unless a default is given as the third argument","The same month's revenue","An error"],"a":1,"w":"There's no previous row. lag(revenue, 1, 0) would return 0 instead."},{"n":6,"ti":"Window Functions","f":"06-window-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which window is right for a 7-row moving average?","o":["OVER (ORDER BY day)","OVER (PARTITION BY day)","OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)","OVER (ORDER BY day RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)"],"a":2,"w":"Six preceding rows plus the current one is seven. (It's seven rows, not seven days: fill missing days first if needed.)"},{"n":7,"ti":"Schema Design & Normalisation","f":"07-normalization.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In enrolments(student_id, course_id, student_name, grade) with key (student_id, course_id), which normal form is violated?","o":["1NF","2NF: student_name depends on only part of the key","3NF","None"],"a":1,"w":"student_id → student_name is a partial dependency on the composite key. Move student_name to students."},{"n":7,"ti":"Schema Design & Normalisation","f":"07-normalization.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"courses(course_id, title, instructor_id, instructor_office): what is the problem?","o":["A partial dependency","A repeating group","A missing primary key","A transitive dependency: course_id → instructor_id → instructor_office (3NF violation)"],"a":3,"w":"The office is a fact about the instructor, not the course. It belongs in instructors(instructor_id, office)."},{"n":7,"ti":"Schema Design & Normalisation","f":"07-normalization.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You can't record a new course until a student enrols in it. Which anomaly is that?","o":["Insert anomaly","Update anomaly","Delete anomaly","Phantom read"],"a":0,"w":"The fact \"course C104 exists\" can't be stored without an unrelated fact (an enrolment), because they share rows."},{"n":7,"ti":"Schema Design & Normalisation","f":"07-normalization.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"order_items stores unit_cents copied from products.price_cents at checkout. Is that a normalisation violation?","o":["Yes, always remove it","Yes, but only in BCNF","No: it records the price agreed at purchase, a different fact that product_id doesn't determine","No, because prices are numbers"],"a":2,"w":"The same product has different prices on different orders, so product_id → unit_cents doesn't hold. Removing it would make old invoices change when prices change."},{"n":7,"ti":"Schema Design & Normalisation","f":"07-normalization.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You add posts.comment_count for a fast feed. What's the most reliable way to keep it correct?","o":["A nightly job only","Update it in the same transaction that inserts or deletes the comment (or with a trigger)","Let the front end compute it","Never update it once set"],"a":1,"w":"Changing the copy in the same transaction as the source fact keeps them consistent. An async or nightly approach can drift and needs a rebuild path."},{"n":8,"ti":"Constraints & Integrity","f":"08-constraints-integrity.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two sign-up requests for the same email arrive 5 ms apart. The application checks \"SELECT … WHERE email = ?\" before inserting, and there's no unique constraint. What can happen?","o":["The second always fails","The database rejects the second insert","Both see no existing row and both insert, creating duplicates","One request deadlocks"],"a":2,"w":"Check-then-act without a lock or constraint is a race. A unique index makes the second insert wait and then fail."},{"n":8,"ti":"Constraints & Integrity","f":"08-constraints-integrity.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Deleting a customer is slow, and EXPLAIN shows a sequential scan on orders. Why?","o":["The foreign-key check looks up orders by customer_id, and that column has no index","customers has no primary key","ON DELETE CASCADE always scans","DELETE can't use indexes"],"a":0,"w":"PostgreSQL indexes the referenced primary key automatically but not the referencing column. Add an index on orders(customer_id)."},{"n":8,"ti":"Constraints & Integrity","f":"08-constraints-integrity.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A column has CHECK (quantity > 0) but no NOT NULL. What happens on INSERT … quantity = NULL ?","o":["Rejected by the CHECK","quantity is set to 0","An error about NULL arithmetic","Accepted: NULL > 0 is unknown, and CHECK only rejects false"],"a":3,"w":"CHECK constraints pass when the condition is true or unknown. Add NOT NULL if a value is required."},{"n":8,"ti":"Constraints & Integrity","f":"08-constraints-integrity.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which constraint prevents two bookings of the same room with overlapping time ranges?","o":["UNIQUE (room_id, start_time)","EXCLUDE USING gist (room_id WITH =, during WITH &&)","CHECK (start_time < end_time)","A foreign key to rooms"],"a":1,"w":"Uniqueness only catches identical values. The exclusion constraint rejects any pair with the same room and overlapping ranges."},{"n":8,"ti":"Constraints & Integrity","f":"08-constraints-integrity.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"UPDATE tracks SET position = position + 1 fails with a duplicate key on (playlist_id, position), although the final state would be valid. What fixes it?","o":["Dropping the primary key","Running it twice","Making the unique constraint DEFERRABLE, so it's checked at the end of the statement","Adding ORDER BY to the UPDATE"],"a":2,"w":"Non-deferrable unique constraints are checked row by row in PostgreSQL. DEFERRABLE (even INITIALLY IMMEDIATE) checks once the statement completes."},{"n":9,"ti":"Indexes: A First Look","f":"09-indexes-first-look.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which query can use an index on (customer_id, created_at) efficiently?","o":["WHERE created_at >= now() - interval '1 day'","WHERE total_cents > 10000","WHERE created_at = $1 OR customer_id = $2","WHERE customer_id = $1 AND created_at >= $2"],"a":3,"w":"The index is sorted by customer_id first. Equality on it, then a range on created_at, reads one contiguous slice."},{"n":9,"ti":"Indexes: A First Look","f":"09-indexes-first-look.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"90% of orders are 'paid'. The planner does a sequential scan for WHERE status = 'paid' despite an index on status. Is that a mistake?","o":["Yes; the index should always be used","No; fetching 90% of rows through the index would mean far more random reads than one sequential pass","Yes; run ANALYZE and it will use the index","No; indexes can't be used for text columns"],"a":1,"w":"Low selectivity: nearly every page would be visited anyway, randomly and repeatedly. The sequential scan is the cheaper plan."},{"n":9,"ti":"Indexes: A First Look","f":"09-indexes-first-look.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does an index-only scan avoid?","o":["Fetching rows from the table, because every needed column is in the index (subject to the visibility map)","Reading the index","Sorting the result","Checking the WHERE clause"],"a":0,"w":"With INCLUDE or key columns covering the query, rows on all-visible pages are answered from the index alone."},{"n":9,"ti":"Indexes: A First Look","f":"09-indexes-first-look.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A table has five secondary indexes. Roughly how many structures does one INSERT update?","o":["1","2","6: the table plus each index","5"],"a":2,"w":"Every index needs an entry for the new row, each a separate B-tree insertion, logged to the WAL."},{"n":9,"ti":"Indexes: A First Look","f":"09-indexes-first-look.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You have indexes on (user_id) and (user_id, created_at). What can you usually do?","o":["Nothing; both are needed","Drop (user_id, created_at)","Merge them with INCLUDE","Drop (user_id): the composite index serves the same lookups, since user_id is its leading column"],"a":3,"w":"A composite index can be used for its leading column alone. The single-column one is usually redundant, unless it's unique and enforcing a rule."},{"n":10,"ti":"Transactions & ACID","f":"10-transactions-acid.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The debit and credit of a transfer run as two autocommit statements, and the server crashes between them. What's the state after restart?","o":["Both changes are undone","The debit is permanent and the credit never happened: money has vanished","Both changes are applied","The database refuses to start"],"a":1,"w":"Each autocommit statement is its own transaction. The first one committed; the second never ran. Wrap both in one transaction."},{"n":10,"ti":"Transactions & ACID","f":"10-transactions-acid.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Inside a PostgreSQL transaction, an INSERT fails a CHECK constraint. You then run another INSERT. What happens?","o":["It succeeds; only the failed statement is undone","It succeeds and the transaction commits automatically","It fails: current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block","The session disconnects"],"a":2,"w":"PostgreSQL aborts the whole transaction on an error. Roll back, or use SAVEPOINT / ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT around statements that may fail."},{"n":10,"ti":"Transactions & ACID","f":"10-transactions-acid.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does COMMIT wait for, by default, before returning?","o":["The WAL to be flushed to disk up to the transaction's commit record","Every modified data page to be written to disk","All replicas to apply the change","The next checkpoint"],"a":0,"w":"Flushing the sequential log is enough for durability; data pages are written later and reconstructed from the WAL after a crash."},{"n":10,"ti":"Transactions & ACID","f":"10-transactions-acid.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With synchronous_commit = off, what can a crash cause?","o":["Half-applied transactions","Corrupted tables","Constraint violations","Loss of the most recent commits that clients were told succeeded, but no partial transactions"],"a":3,"w":"Commits return before the WAL flush, so the last moments of acknowledged commits can be lost. Atomicity and consistency are unaffected."},{"n":10,"ti":"Transactions & ACID","f":"10-transactions-acid.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which ACID property depends most on the application rather than the database?","o":["Atomicity","Durability","Consistency","Isolation"],"a":2,"w":"The database enforces declared constraints; whether a transaction preserves the business's invariants (money moved in equals money moved out) is up to its logic."},{"n":11,"ti":"Pages, Tuples & Heap Files","f":"11-pages-heap-files.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"An index entry stores a row's address as (page 7, line pointer 3). Why a line pointer number and not a byte offset in the page?","o":["Line pointer numbers are smaller","The tuple can then be moved within the page, to close up gaps, without changing its address","Byte offsets can't be stored in an index","It makes the page smaller"],"a":1,"w":"Only the offset inside the line pointer changes. Nothing outside the page has to be updated."},{"n":11,"ti":"Pages, Tuples & Heap Files","f":"11-pages-heap-files.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"You run DELETE on a row. What happens on its page straight away?","o":["The tuple's bytes are zeroed","The tuple is removed and the others slide together","The page is rewritten without it","The tuple's xmax is set. The bytes stay until VACUUM or pruning removes them"],"a":3,"w":"Other transactions may still need to see the row, so it can't be removed yet."},{"n":11,"ti":"Pages, Tuples & Heap Files","f":"11-pages-heap-files.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A table has fillfactor 100 and its pages are full. A row is updated, changing a column that has no index. What does the update cost?","o":["The new version goes to another page, and every index on the table gets a new entry","Nothing extra: it is a HOT update","The row is overwritten in place","The update fails with \"page full\""],"a":0,"w":"A HOT update needs room on the same page. Without it, the row moves and the indexes must follow."},{"n":11,"ti":"Pages, Tuples & Heap Files","f":"11-pages-heap-files.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A row contains a 50 kB text value. Where is it stored?","o":["In the row, which spans seven pages","In a separate file, one per value","Compressed, and if still too large, in chunks in the table's TOAST table, with a pointer left in the row","It can't be stored: rows are limited to 8 kB"],"a":2,"w":"A tuple can't span pages, so large values are moved out of line."},{"n":11,"ti":"Pages, Tuples & Heap Files","f":"11-pages-heap-files.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which table gains most from a fillfactor of 75?","o":["An append-only table of events","A sessions table whose rows are updated many times a minute","A lookup table of 50 countries","A table that is truncated and reloaded every night"],"a":1,"w":"Reserved space lets frequent updates stay on their page, as HOT updates. Tables that aren't updated never use it."},{"n":12,"ti":"The Buffer Pool","f":"12-buffer-pool.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A frame has a pin count of 2. What does that mean for eviction?","o":["It is evicted before frames with lower counts","It has been modified twice","Two processes are using the page now, so it can't be evicted","It will be written to disk twice"],"a":2,"w":"A pin promises that the page stays in its frame while someone is reading or changing it."},{"n":12,"ti":"The Buffer Pool","f":"12-buffer-pool.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A transaction updates a row and commits. When is the changed table page written to its file?","o":["Later: by the checkpointer or background writer, or when the frame is needed. COMMIT waited only for the WAL","Before COMMIT returns","Immediately after each UPDATE statement","Only when the server shuts down"],"a":0,"w":"The WAL makes the change durable. The page stays dirty in memory and may absorb many more changes before it is written once."},{"n":12,"ti":"The Buffer Pool","f":"12-buffer-pool.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Under plain LRU, what does one scan of a very large table do to the buffer pool?","o":["Nothing: scans don't use the pool","It evicts only pages that are dirty","It is refused if the pool is full","Every scanned page becomes \"most recently used\", and the pages the application needs all the time are pushed out"],"a":3,"w":"Recency alone can't tell a page read once from a page read a thousand times."},{"n":12,"ti":"The Buffer Pool","f":"12-buffer-pool.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In PostgreSQL's clock sweep, the hand reaches an unpinned frame with a usage count of 3. What happens?","o":["The frame is evicted","The count is reduced to 2 and the hand moves on","The count is reset to 0 and the frame is evicted","The hand stops and waits"],"a":1,"w":"Only a frame found at 0 is evicted. Pages used often are passed over several times."},{"n":12,"ti":"The Buffer Pool","f":"12-buffer-pool.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is shared_buffers usually about 25% of RAM for PostgreSQL, and not 80%?","o":["PostgreSQL can't address more memory","Larger pools make every query slower","PostgreSQL also uses the OS page cache, which needs memory, as do sorts and connections","The rest is reserved for the WAL"],"a":2,"w":"Pages that leave the pool are often still in the OS cache. InnoDB, which bypasses that cache, is the one that takes 70 to 80%."},{"n":13,"ti":"B-Trees","f":"13-b-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"An index page holds about 367 entries. Roughly how many levels does a B-tree over 1 billion rows have?","o":["2","4","30","367"],"a":1,"w":"367³ is about 49 million and 367⁴ about 18 billion, so four levels are enough."},{"n":13,"ti":"B-Trees","f":"13-b-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is a database index a tree of wide pages and not a balanced binary tree?","o":["Binary trees can't be balanced","Wide pages need fewer comparisons","Binary trees can't store duplicates","The cost is page reads, and a wide node gets hundreds of branches out of each one"],"a":3,"w":"Thirty levels would be thirty page reads. Four levels is four, and most of those are cached."},{"n":13,"ti":"B-Trees","f":"13-b-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A key is inserted into a leaf page that is full. What happens?","o":["The page splits: some entries move to a new page, and the parent gains a separator pointing to it","The insert fails until VACUUM has run","The key is put in the parent page instead","The whole index is rebuilt"],"a":0,"w":"If the parent is full as well, it splits too, and so on up to the root."},{"n":13,"ti":"B-Trees","f":"13-b-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How does a B+ tree answer WHERE created_at BETWEEN a AND b ?","o":["A separate search from the root for every key in the range","A scan of every leaf in the index","One descent to the first key, then a walk along the linked leaves until the end of the range","It can't: B-trees only support equality"],"a":2,"w":"The leaves hold all the keys in order and are linked to their neighbours."},{"n":13,"ti":"B-Trees","f":"13-b-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A large table has a random UUID as its primary key. Why are its inserts slower than with a serial key?","o":["UUIDs take longer to compare","Each insert lands on a random leaf, which may not be in memory, and splits happen all over the index","UUID indexes are hash indexes","The tree becomes unbalanced"],"a":1,"w":"Serial keys all go to the rightmost leaf, which is always cached and fills densely."},{"n":14,"ti":"LSM Trees","f":"14-lsm-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does an LSM tree write to disk before acknowledging a put?","o":["The page of the SSTable that holds the key","A new SSTable","One append to the write-ahead log","Nothing"],"a":2,"w":"The log makes the write durable, and the memtable holds it in sorted order until the next flush."},{"n":14,"ti":"LSM Trees","f":"14-lsm-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How does an LSM tree delete a key whose value is in an SSTable?","o":["It writes a tombstone, and the old value is physically removed later by compaction","It rewrites the SSTable without the key","It overwrites the value with zeros in place","It removes the key from the file's index block"],"a":0,"w":"SSTables are never modified. The tombstone hides older entries until a merge can drop them all."},{"n":14,"ti":"LSM Trees","f":"14-lsm-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A Bloom filter for an SSTable says a key \"might be present\". What do you know?","o":["The key is in the file","The key is not in the file","The filter is corrupt","The file has to be read to find out. Only a \"no\" from the filter is certain"],"a":3,"w":"Bloom filters have false positives, at a rate of about 1% with 10 bits a key, and no false negatives."},{"n":14,"ti":"LSM Trees","f":"14-lsm-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Compaction is falling behind on a write-heavy LSM store. What happens first?","o":["Data is lost","Files accumulate, so reads check more of them and space fills with old versions. In the end writes are throttled or stalled","Writes get faster, as nothing competes with them","The memtable grows without limit"],"a":1,"w":"The store slows writers on purpose, to stop the read path from getting worse without bound."},{"n":14,"ti":"LSM Trees","f":"14-lsm-trees.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which workload suits an LSM tree better than a B-tree?","o":["Mostly long range scans over a table that rarely changes","A small table that fits in memory and is read constantly","A very high rate of small writes with random keys, on a data set much larger than memory","Queries that need the lowest and steadiest read latency"],"a":2,"w":"Random-key writes are a B-tree's worst case and an LSM tree's ordinary one."},{"n":15,"ti":"Hash, GIN, GiST & Bitmap Indexes","f":"15-other-indexes.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which index supports WHERE tags @> ARRAY['sale', 'new'] on a text[] column?","o":["B-tree","Hash","GIN","BRIN"],"a":2,"w":"GIN indexes each array element with the rows that contain it, and intersects the lists for 'sale' and 'new'."},{"n":15,"ti":"Hash, GIN, GiST & Bitmap Indexes","f":"15-other-indexes.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is inserting into a table with a full-text GIN index slower than into one with a B-tree?","o":["One row contributes an index entry for every distinct word in it, where a B-tree gets one entry","GIN indexes aren't logged","GIN has to lock the whole table","GIN stores the full text twice"],"a":0,"w":"The pending list defers this work and does not remove it."},{"n":15,"ti":"Hash, GIN, GiST & Bitmap Indexes","f":"15-other-indexes.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A BRIN index on created_at performs very well on one events table and uselessly on another of the same size. What is the likely difference?","o":["The second table has more columns","The second table's index was built with fewer pages in each range","The second table has no primary key","The second table's rows aren't stored in created_at order, so every block range spans nearly all dates"],"a":3,"w":"BRIN can only skip a block range whose minimum and maximum exclude the value being sought."},{"n":15,"ti":"Hash, GIN, GiST & Bitmap Indexes","f":"15-other-indexes.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which index can return \"the ten rows nearest to this point\", already in order of distance?","o":["Hash","GiST","BRIN","A B-tree on (x, y)"],"a":1,"w":"GiST supports ordering by the distance operator. A B-tree on two coordinates is sorted by x first, which says little about distance."},{"n":15,"ti":"Hash, GIN, GiST & Bitmap Indexes","f":"15-other-indexes.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In a plan, what does BitmapAnd over two Bitmap Index Scans do?","o":["Joins two tables","Merges two indexes into one on disk","Intersects the sets of row addresses from two indexes, so the table is visited only for rows matching both conditions","Sorts the rows by both columns"],"a":2,"w":"Each index produces a bitmap. The AND of the two is then used for one ordered pass over the table."},{"n":16,"ti":"How a Query Runs","f":"16-query-processing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"At which stage is ERROR: column \"custmer_id\" does not exist raised?","o":["Parsing","Analysis, when names are looked up in the catalogue","Planning","Execution"],"a":1,"w":"The statement is grammatical, so the parser accepts it. The analyser is the first stage that knows what columns exist."},{"n":16,"ti":"How a Query Runs","f":"16-query-processing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A query selects from a view with a WHERE clause. What does the planner see?","o":["The view's stored results, to which the filter is applied","Two separate queries, run one after the other","A temporary table created from the view","One query over the base tables, with the view's definition substituted in"],"a":3,"w":"The rewriter expands the view, so the filter can be pushed down to the tables and their indexes."},{"n":16,"ti":"How a Query Runs","f":"16-query-processing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In the iterator model, what does the executor do to get a result row?","o":["It asks the root operator for its next row, and each operator asks its children in turn","It runs each operator to completion, from the bottom up","It pushes every table row up through the tree","It runs all operators in parallel"],"a":0,"w":"Rows are pulled on demand, one at a time, which is what lets an operator such as Limit stop the ones below it."},{"n":16,"ti":"How a Query Runs","f":"16-query-processing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"SELECT … ORDER BY total DESC LIMIT 3 runs on a table with no index on total . How many rows does the scan read?","o":["3","About 3, depending on the data","All of them: Sort needs its whole input before it can return the first row","None: the planner refuses the query"],"a":2,"w":"Sort is a blocking operator, so the Limit above it can't cut the scan short."},{"n":16,"ti":"How a Query Runs","f":"16-query-processing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why do analytical databases pass batches of column values between operators and not single rows?","o":["Batches use less memory","The overhead of each call is paid once for a thousand values, and the inner loops become simple array operations","Single rows can't be aggregated","It makes transactions safer"],"a":1,"w":"For a scan of a billion values, the interpretation overhead for each row is most of the cost."},{"n":17,"ti":"Reading EXPLAIN","f":"17-explain.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A node shows (actual time=0.150..0.200 rows=2 loops=30000) . How long did it take, and how many rows did it return, in total?","o":["0.2 ms and 2 rows","0.2 ms and 60,000 rows","About 6 seconds and 60,000 rows","About 4.5 seconds and 2 rows"],"a":2,"w":"Both figures are for one run: 0.2 ms × 30,000 is 6 s, and 2 × 30,000 is 60,000 rows."},{"n":17,"ti":"Reading EXPLAIN","f":"17-explain.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A line reads Seq Scan on orders (… rows=98 …) (actual … rows=103 loops=1) , and under it Rows Removed by Filter: 4999897 . What does it tell you?","o":["The scan read five million rows to return 103: an index on the filtered column is missing","The estimate is badly wrong","The table needs to be vacuumed","The filter removed duplicates"],"a":0,"w":"The estimate, 98 against 103, is good. The waste is in how the rows were found."},{"n":17,"ti":"Reading EXPLAIN","f":"17-explain.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does a row estimate of 1 with an actual count of 50,000 so often go with a very slow query?","o":["The planner stops planning when it sees 1","Rows are fetched one at a time","It means the statistics are locked","Expecting one row, the planner chooses a nested loop, and the inner side then runs 50,000 times"],"a":3,"w":"A nested loop is the best join for one outer row and among the worst for fifty thousand."},{"n":17,"ti":"Reading EXPLAIN","f":"17-explain.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In Buffers: shared hit=120 read=48000 , what does read=48000 mean?","o":["48,000 rows were read","48,000 pages weren't in shared_buffers and had to be requested from the operating system","48,000 bytes were read from disk","The query was run 48,000 times"],"a":1,"w":"Buffers are pages of 8 kB, so this is about 375 MB brought into the buffer pool."},{"n":17,"ti":"Reading EXPLAIN","f":"17-explain.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A plan contains Sort Method: external merge Disk: 117160kB . What happened, and what is the usual remedy?","o":["The sort used an index. Nothing to do","The disk is failing. Replace it","The sort didn't fit in work_mem and spilled to temporary files. Raise work_mem for the query, sort fewer rows, or use an index that supplies the order","The table is bloated. Run VACUUM FULL"],"a":2,"w":"An in-memory sort is reported as quicksort or top-N heapsort, with its memory use."},{"n":18,"ti":"Join Algorithms","f":"18-join-algorithms.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A query joins 5 rows of customers to their rows in orders , which has 50 million rows and an index on customer_id . Which join is best?","o":["Hash join","Nested loop, using the index","Merge join","They cost the same"],"a":1,"w":"Five index lookups, about twenty page reads. A hash join would read all 50 million rows."},{"n":18,"ti":"Join Algorithms","f":"18-join-algorithms.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two tables of 10 million rows each are joined on equality. Neither is sorted, and no index is useful. What will the planner most likely choose?","o":["A nested loop","An index nested loop","A merge join","A hash join"],"a":3,"w":"It reads each input once. A merge join would have to sort 20 million rows first."},{"n":18,"ti":"Join Algorithms","f":"18-join-algorithms.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which join can evaluate ON a.start_time < b.end_time ?","o":["Only the nested loop","Only the hash join","The hash join and the merge join","All three"],"a":0,"w":"Hashing only finds equal keys. The nested loop tests any condition, pair by pair."},{"n":18,"ti":"Join Algorithms","f":"18-join-algorithms.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A plan shows Hash … Buckets: 131072 Batches: 32 Memory Usage: 4097kB . What does Batches: 32 say?","o":["The join used 32 parallel workers","The hash table had 32 collisions","The hash table didn't fit in work_mem , so the inputs were split into 32 parts on disk and joined a part at a time","The join returned 32 rows"],"a":2,"w":"A hash join that fits in memory shows Batches: 1."},{"n":18,"ti":"Join Algorithms","f":"18-join-algorithms.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"You write FROM a JOIN b … JOIN c … . In what order does PostgreSQL join the three tables?","o":["Always a, then b, then c, as written","In whichever order it estimates to be cheapest","Largest table first","Alphabetically"],"a":1,"w":"For inner joins the result is the same in any order, so the planner is free to choose. The written order is kept only beyond the collapse limit."},{"n":19,"ti":"The Cost-Based Optimiser","f":"19-cost-based-optimizer.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A column's statistics list 'pending' among the most common values with frequency 0.02. The table has 5 million rows. How many rows does the planner expect for status = 'pending' ?","o":["2","20,000","100,000","1,000,000"],"a":2,"w":"For a value in the common-values list, the estimate is its recorded frequency times the row count."},{"n":19,"ti":"The Cost-Based Optimiser","f":"19-cost-based-optimizer.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A query has WHERE city = 'Leeds' AND postcode LIKE 'LS%' . Each condition alone matches 1% of rows, and they match the same rows. What does the planner estimate, without extended statistics?","o":["0.01%: it multiplies the two, a hundred times too low","1%, correctly","2%","It refuses to estimate"],"a":0,"w":"The planner assumes that conditions on different columns are independent."},{"n":19,"ti":"The Cost-Based Optimiser","f":"19-cost-based-optimizer.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Straight after 10 million rows are bulk-loaded into a table that held 10,000, queries on it get bad plans. What is the first thing to do?","o":["Add indexes","Increase work_mem","Disable sequential scans","Run ANALYZE on the table"],"a":3,"w":"The stored statistics still describe the small table. Autovacuum would get to it eventually, and the load should not wait for that."},{"n":19,"ti":"The Cost-Based Optimiser","f":"19-cost-based-optimizer.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does WHERE total_cents / 100 > 5000 get a worse estimate than WHERE total_cents > 500000 ?","o":["Division is slow","The histogram describes total_cents , not total_cents / 100 , so the planner falls back to a fixed guess","The first form returns different rows","Integer division loses precision"],"a":1,"w":"Keep the bare column on one side of the comparison, and the planner can use its statistics and its indexes."},{"n":19,"ti":"The Cost-Based Optimiser","f":"19-cost-based-optimizer.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"According to the 2015 study of query optimisers, what matters most for getting a good plan?","o":["A more detailed cost model","Trying more join orders","Accurate estimates of row counts","More indexes"],"a":2,"w":"Given the true row counts, even a simple cost model chose plans close to the best."},{"n":20,"ti":"WAL & Crash Recovery","f":"20-wal-recovery.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A transaction commits, and the server loses power before any of the pages it changed have been written to the data files. What happens on restart?","o":["The transaction is lost","Recovery replays its log records, and the changes are there","The database is corrupt","The client is asked to resend it"],"a":1,"w":"COMMIT returned only after the log was flushed, and the log is enough to rebuild the pages."},{"n":20,"ti":"WAL & Crash Recovery","f":"20-wal-recovery.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Before the buffer manager writes a dirty page to its data file, what must be true?","o":["The transaction that changed it has committed","A checkpoint is in progress","No other transaction is reading the page","The log has been flushed at least as far as the page's LSN"],"a":3,"w":"The page may hold uncommitted changes. What matters is that the log can explain whatever is on disk."},{"n":20,"ti":"WAL & Crash Recovery","f":"20-wal-recovery.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"During recovery, a log record has LSN 500 and the page it refers to has LSN 620. What does recovery do?","o":["Skips the record: the page already contains that change","Applies the record","Reports corruption","Rolls the page back to LSN 500"],"a":0,"w":"The page was written after the change was made. Applying it again could be wrong, and the LSN test prevents that."},{"n":20,"ti":"WAL & Crash Recovery","f":"20-wal-recovery.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does a checkpoint achieve?","o":["It commits all open transactions","It makes a backup of the database","It writes all dirty pages to the data files, so that recovery can start from that point and older log can be recycled","It empties the buffer pool"],"a":2,"w":"Without checkpoints, recovery would have to replay the whole history of the database."},{"n":20,"ti":"WAL & Crash Recovery","f":"20-wal-recovery.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does the volume of WAL rise sharply just after each checkpoint?","o":["The checkpoint itself is written to the log many times","The first change to each page after a checkpoint logs a full image of the page, to guard against torn writes","Transactions queue up during the checkpoint","The log is rewritten from the start"],"a":1,"w":"An 8 kB image in place of a record of a few dozen bytes, once for each page in each checkpoint cycle."},{"n":21,"ti":"Isolation Levels & Anomalies","f":"21-isolation-anomalies.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two transactions read stock = 10. One writes 7 and commits, then the other writes 8 and commits, at Read Committed. What is this called, and does the database report it?","o":["A dirty read, and an error is raised","A phantom, and a warning is logged","A lost update, and nothing is reported","A deadlock, and one transaction is aborted"],"a":2,"w":"Both transactions succeed. The first one's write is silently overwritten."},{"n":21,"ti":"Isolation Levels & Anomalies","f":"21-isolation-anomalies.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In PostgreSQL at Read Committed, a transaction runs the same SELECT twice and gets different results. Why?","o":["Each statement sees what was committed when that statement began, and another transaction committed in between","The first SELECT read uncommitted data","The table has no primary key","The planner chose a different plan"],"a":0,"w":"This is a non-repeatable read, or a phantom if the set of rows changed. Repeatable Read uses one snapshot for the whole transaction."},{"n":21,"ti":"Isolation Levels & Anomalies","f":"21-isolation-anomalies.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"At Repeatable Read in PostgreSQL, a transaction tries to UPDATE a row that another transaction changed and committed after the first one's snapshot. What happens?","o":["The update is applied to the old version","The update silently overwrites the other change","The transaction waits for ever","It fails with \"could not serialize access due to concurrent update\", and should be retried"],"a":3,"w":"The first writer wins. That is how this level prevents lost updates."},{"n":21,"ti":"Isolation Levels & Anomalies","f":"21-isolation-anomalies.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why doesn't snapshot isolation prevent write skew?","o":["Snapshots are taken too late","The two transactions write different rows, so there is no write conflict for it to detect","It only applies to SELECT statements","It allows dirty reads"],"a":1,"w":"The conflict is between one transaction's read and the other's write, which only Serializable tracks."},{"n":21,"ti":"Isolation Levels & Anomalies","f":"21-isolation-anomalies.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A transaction at Serializable fails with a serialisation failure. What should the application do?","o":["Report the error to the user","Re-run the last statement","Run the whole transaction again from its first statement","Lower the isolation level and try again"],"a":2,"w":"Its reads are out of date, so everything decided from them has to be decided again."},{"n":22,"ti":"Locking & Two-Phase Locking","f":"22-two-phase-locking.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Under strict two-phase locking, when does a transaction release the lock on a row it updated?","o":["As soon as the UPDATE statement finishes","When the transaction commits or rolls back","When another transaction asks for it","After a timeout"],"a":1,"w":"Every lock is held to the end of the transaction, however long that is."},{"n":22,"ti":"Locking & Two-Phase Locking","f":"22-two-phase-locking.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In PostgreSQL, session 1 has updated row 5 and not committed. Session 2 runs a plain SELECT that reads row 5. What happens?","o":["It waits for session 1 to commit","It fails with a lock error","It returns the uncommitted value","It returns at once, with the last committed version of the row"],"a":3,"w":"Plain reads take no row locks. They read an older version (Chapter 23)."},{"n":22,"ti":"Locking & Two-Phase Locking","f":"22-two-phase-locking.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Ten workers each run SELECT … FROM jobs WHERE status = 'queued' ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE . What happens, and what fixes it?","o":["All ten queue for the same row and work one at a time. Add SKIP LOCKED","They deadlock. Add NOWAIT","Each gets a different job. Nothing to fix","All ten take the same job. Add a unique index"],"a":0,"w":"SKIP LOCKED passes over rows that another transaction has locked, so each worker gets the next free job."},{"n":22,"ti":"Locking & Two-Phase Locking","f":"22-two-phase-locking.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"An ALTER TABLE is waiting for its lock behind a long-running transaction. Why do new SELECT s on the table now hang?","o":["The table is being rewritten","SELECTs conflict with the long-running transaction","They queue behind the waiting ALTER TABLE, whose requested lock conflicts with theirs","The connection pool is misconfigured"],"a":2,"w":"Lock requests are served in order. A pending request for an exclusive lock holds up everything that arrives after it."},{"n":22,"ti":"Locking & Two-Phase Locking","f":"22-two-phase-locking.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is lock escalation, and does PostgreSQL do it?","o":["Raising the isolation level automatically. Yes","Replacing many row locks with one page or table lock to save memory. No: PostgreSQL keeps row locks in the rows","Giving priority to older transactions. Yes","Turning shared locks into exclusive ones. Yes"],"a":1,"w":"SQL Server escalates at about 5,000 row locks. PostgreSQL has no need to."}];
